--- body ---
## Context

Author directive, 2026-10-26: the orchestrator decides how much review a change
needs; gaze executes that decision. This is the third attempt, and the ticket
records why the second did not take.

Attempt 1 (tracker [[1100]], children 1142, 1154): hunt owns a proportionate
review plan with a risk floor; gaze executes it. Attempt 2 (note of 2026-10-21,
ticket 1176): "trust the orchestrator, no floor, no category" deleted the floor
and superseded the child that had gaze execute a plan, leaving "the gaze
controller chooses seats and depth". That moved the decision inside gaze. The
caller, which knows the diff size, the risk, what was already reviewed and what
changed since, has no way to say anything: gaze's input is
`<pr-number> [--force-approve <reason>]` and nothing else, so it defaults to
its full panel.

Incident (2026-10-26): PR 1667, a three-line addition and fifteen-line deletion
of one paragraph of `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` the author had already agreed to, got
a five-seat panel, then a gate, and took about ten minutes to land.

Alignment (2026-10-26, author: "yes, align"): the brief is the inline form of
the request and response files deferred by ticket 1154 (tracker [[1100]], dyne
format, git-erg 0271 to 0275): the brief is the frozen request, the result
comment that echoes it is the response. Ticket [[1223]] is folded in here: the
tier stays purely mechanical, and memory files that steer sessions get a
default brief instead of a full-tier floor.

Related: [[1100]], [[1219]] (gaze prescribes no caller behaviour), [[1221]]
(proportional merge gating), [[1224]] (print durations).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `/gaze` accepts a caller brief next to the PR number: which perspectives to run (including none beyond mechanical adherence) and the scope to review (the whole diff, or a commit range or path list); it runs exactly that, with no seat added and no seat dropped on its own.
- [ ] The result comment states the brief it received and what ran against it, so the caller sees one line to compare; a brief gaze cannot honour (unknown seat, scope outside the diff) is refused with a reason, never silently widened.
- [ ] Gaze keeps a default brief only for a caller that sends none (an interactive author running `/gaze <pr>`); `/raid`, `/hunt` and `/merge` each say where the brief is decided (the orchestrator) and point to one place that tells how to size it.
- [ ] "No review" for bookkeeping paths (`skills/merge/SKILL.md` § Gating tiers) becomes a default the orchestrator may override in the brief: the tier decides only which checks the mechanical record runs, and an orchestrator that knows a bookkeeping path steers later sessions (for example `memory/MEMORY.md`) may ask for a review of it. The tier is not used to force review: `memory/MEMORY.md` and `memory/topics/` stay bookkeeping for the record's checks (ticket [[1223]] folded in).
- [ ] The sizing guidance lives in one home, as judgement for the orchestrator and not a mechanical floor or category (attempts 1 and 2 are not repeated), with at least the worked cases: one-paragraph prose deletion, rebase or merge of main only, fix of listed findings, large multi-file change, and an edit of `memory/MEMORY.md` or `memory/topics/` (files every session reads as guidance: one adherence seat by default, the orchestrator may widen; from ticket 1223).
- [ ] A regression test shows gaze run with a one-seat brief launches one seat, and with a scope brief reviews only that range.
- [ ] The harness gate (`.merge-gate.json` checks) passes.

